The Code Mode tool catalog has changed. This catalog supersedes the previous Code Mode tool catalog.

# Code Mode

The tools listed below, and `search`, cannot be called directly: they only work as JavaScript inside the `execute` tool. Every other tool you have is a normal tool call: call it directly, never inside `execute`.

The catalog is partial. Before calling a tool that is not listed below, find it with the global `search(...)` function. Do not guess tool names. Each result's `path` is the complete call expression: write it out as code, for example `await tools.ns.tool_name({ ... })`. Do not use it as a key such as `tools[path]`.

- search({
  query?: string,
  namespace?: string,
  /** @integer @exclusiveMinimum 0 */
  limit?: number,
  /** @integer @minimum 0 */
  offset?: number,
}): {
  items: Array<{
      path: string,
      description: string,
      signature: string,
    }>,
  /** @integer @minimum 0 */
  remaining: number,
  next: {
      /** @integer @minimum 0 */
      offset: number,
    } | null,
}

## Code Mode tools (call only inside `execute`)

- context-mode (11 tools, 1 shown)
  - tools["context-mode"].ctx_doctor(): Promise<unknown> // Diagnose context-mode installation. Runs all checks server-side and returns a plain-text status report with [OK]/[FAI...
- ctx_batch_execute (1 tool)
  - tools.ctx_batch_execute({
  /**
   * Commands to execute as a batch. Output is labeled with the section header. Default order is sequential; pass concurrency>1 to run in parallel (output stays in input order).
   */
  commands: Array<{
      /** Section header for this command's output (e.g., 'README', 'Package.json', 'Source Tree') */
      label: string,
      /** Shell command to execute */
      command: string,
    }>,
  /**
   * Search queries to extract information from indexed output. Use 5-8 comprehensive queries. Each returns top 5 matching sections with full content. This is your ONLY chance — put ALL your questions here. No follow-up calls needed.
   */
  queries: Array<string>,
  /**
   * Max execution time in ms. When omitted, no server-side timer fires — the MCP host's RPC timeout governs. With concurrency=1, the value (when set) is a shared budget across commands; with concurrency>1, it is applied per-command.
   */
  timeout?: number,
  /**
   * Max commands to run in parallel (1-8, default: 1). Use 4-8 for I/O-bound batches (network, gh, curl, multi-repo git reads). Keep at 1 for CPU-bound (npm test, build, lint) or stateful commands (ports, locks). >1 switches to per-command timeouts (no shared budget) and individual `(timed out)` blocks instead of cascading skip.
   * @default 1
   */
  concurrency?: number,
  /** Optional working directory for all shell commands in this batch. */
  cwd?: string,
  /**
   * Scope for `queries` (default: `batch`). `batch` searches ONLY the chunks produced by this batch's commands — useful when you want answers about the just-fetched output. `global` searches the entire persistent index (same scope as ctx_search) — useful when you want the batch commands to enrich context and the queries to also surface related prior knowledge in one round trip.
   * @default "batch"
   */
  query_scope?: "batch" | "global",
}): Promise<string | null> // Run multiple commands in ONE call. Every command's output is auto-indexed into the knowledge base; if you also pass `...
- ctx_doctor (1 tool)
  - tools.ctx_doctor(): Promise<string | null> // Diagnose context-mode installation. Runs all checks server-side and returns a plain-text status report with [OK]/[FAI...
- ctx_execute (1 tool)
  - tools.ctx_execute({
  /** Runtime language */
  language: "javascript" | "typescript" | "python" | "shell" | "ruby" | "go" | "rust" | "php" | "perl" | "r" | "elixir" | "csharp",
  /**
   * Source code to execute. Use console.log (JS/TS), print (Python/Ruby/Perl/R), echo (Shell), echo (PHP), fmt.Println (Go), IO.puts (Elixir), or Console.WriteLine (C#) to output a summary to context.
   */
  code: string,
  /**
   * Max execution time in ms. When omitted, no server-side timer fires — the MCP host's RPC timeout governs (which is the right layer for this policy). Pass an explicit value for long-running builds (Gradle/Maven/SBT).
   */
  timeout?: number,
  /**
   * Keep process running after timeout (for servers/daemons). Returns partial output without killing the process. IMPORTANT: Do NOT add setTimeout/self-close timers in background scripts — the process must stay alive until the timeout detaches it. For server+fetch patterns, prefer putting both server and fetch in ONE ctx_execute call instead of using background.
   */
  background?: boolean,
  /**
   * Optional working directory for shell commands. Non-shell languages still execute from their sandbox temp directory.
   */
  cwd?: string,
  /**
   * What you're looking for in the output. When provided and output is large (>5KB), indexes output into knowledge base and returns section titles + previews — not full content. Use ctx_search(queries: [...]) to retrieve specific sections. Example: 'failing tests', 'HTTP 500 errors'.
   *
   * TIP: Use specific technical terms, not just concepts. Check 'Searchable terms' in the response for available vocabulary.
   */
  intent?: string,
}): Promise<string | null> // Run code in a sandboxed subprocess. Languages: javascript, shell, python, ruby, rust, perl.
- ctx_execute_file (1 tool)
  - tools.ctx_execute_file({
  /** Absolute file path or relative to project root */
  path: string,
  /** Runtime language */
  language: "javascript" | "typescript" | "python" | "shell" | "ruby" | "go" | "rust" | "php" | "perl" | "r" | "elixir" | "csharp",
  /**
   * Code to process FILE_CONTENT (file_content in Elixir). Print summary via console.log/print/echo/IO.puts/Console.WriteLine.
   */
  code: string,
  /** Max execution time in ms. When omitted, no server-side timer fires — the MCP host's RPC timeout governs. */
  timeout?: number,
  /**
   * What you're looking for in the output. When provided and output is large (>5KB), returns only matching sections via BM25 search instead of truncated output.
   */
  intent?: string,
}): Promise<string | null> // Read a file into a sandboxed FILE_CONTENT variable and run code over it. Only what you console.log() enters your conv...
- ctx_fetch_and_index (1 tool, none shown)
- ctx_index (1 tool, none shown)
- ctx_insight (1 tool)
  - tools.ctx_insight(): Promise<string | null> // Opens the context-mode Insight dashboard (https://context-mode.com/insight) in your default browser — a dashboard lau...
- ctx_purge (1 tool)
  - tools.ctx_purge({
  /** MUST be true. Destructive operation; false returns 'purge cancelled'. */
  confirm: boolean,
  /**
   * UUID of a single session. Pairs with confirm:true to wipe only that session's events + per-session FTS5 chunks. Sibling sessions and the stats file are preserved. MUST NOT be combined with scope:'project'.
   */
  sessionId?: string,
  /**
   * Explicit scope selector. 'session' REQUIRES sessionId. 'project' wipes the entire project (FTS5 + every session + stats). Omit only for the deprecated bare-{confirm:true} back-compat path.
   */
  scope?: "session" | "project",
}): Promise<string | null> // DESTRUCTIVE: permanently delete indexed content. Cannot be undone. Requires confirm:true and exactly one scope.
- ctx_search (1 tool, none shown)
- ctx_stats (1 tool)
  - tools.ctx_stats(): Promise<string | null> // Returns context consumption statistics for the current session. Shows total bytes returned to context, breakdown by t...
- ctx_upgrade (1 tool, none shown)
- headroom (3 tools, none shown)
- mnemolite (9 tools, none shown)
- opencode (8 tools, 4 shown) // Tools for managing OpenCode itself, such as working with sessions and worktrees, searching the available models, and reading MCP resources.
  - tools.opencode.session_move({
  /** Omit to move the current session. @pattern ^ses */
  sessionID?: string,
  /** Destination directory, relative to the target session's directory or absolute. Supports ~. @minLength 1 */
  directory: string,
}): Promise<{
  /** @pattern ^ses */
  sessionID: string,
  directory: string,
}> // Move a session to another directory, or omit sessionID to move the current session. The current session moves at the ...
  - tools.opencode.worktree_create({
  name: string,
}): Promise<{
  directory: string,
}> // Create a named worktree. Returns its directory.
  - tools.opencode.worktree_list(): Promise<{
  worktrees: Array<{
      directory: string,
      strategy?: string,
    }>,
}> // List a repository's worktrees.
  - tools.opencode.worktree_remove({
  /** Absolute path of the worktree to remove. @minLength 1 */
  directory: string,
  /** Discard modified and untracked files. Only set this when the user has confirmed forced removal. */
  force?: boolean,
}): Promise<{
  directory: string,
  removed: boolean,
}> // Remove a worktree. The repository location selects configuration; directory identifies the worktree to remove.

